    "result_text": "Green. Done.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline already let `sharesHousehold: true` requests bypass the duplicate-household rejection (`RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold`). What was missing was the shared, returnable `householdId`. I added it as a **derived** value rather than stored state, so two owners at the same last name + address inherently resolve to the same id.\n\nThree files touched, each a small cohesive unit:\n\n1. **`HouseholdId.java`** (new) \u2014 a tiny stateless helper that hashes the owner's normalized last name + address (same case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed grouping `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold` uses) into a stable hex identifier. New class \u21d2 no surrounding class complexity to carry.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` line emitting `householdId` into the response DTO. No method body grew.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declares `householdId` as a read-only field on the `Owner` response schema, so the generated DTO serializes it.\n\n## Why derived, not a persisted column\n\nThe request's `sharesHousehold` is `@Transient`, so it isn't available on the `GET /api/owners/{id}` read-back the test uses. Deriving `householdId` deterministically from the persisted last name + address makes it \"a stable shared identifier\" available on every read without adding a DB column, an entity field, a new pipeline step, or schema-file edits across four databases. That kept the change off the large `Owner` entity (high surrounding complexity) and the multi-file schema set \u2014 the biggest structural-cost levers.\n\nAny two owners in the same household (same last name + address) compute the same id, so the opted-in joiners share it; the acceptance test and full suite pass.",
